# Two modules on different go directives, each answered at its own.
#
# example.com/foo at the root declares go 1.21.0 and example.com/bar under sub/
# declares go 1.22.0. Both call net/http. The database holds two standard-library
# advisories over different ranges: GO-TEST-9001 below go1.21.9, and GO-TEST-9002
# from go1.22.0 up to go1.22.5.
#
# So the two modules are affected by one advisory each, and move to different
# versions: the root to 1.21.9 and sub/ to 1.22.5. Neither is affected by the
# other's advisory, which is what makes the pair worth scanning separately.
#
# Reading the standard library at the toolchain running the scan cannot produce
# this: one toolchain answers both modules the same way, and whatever Go is
# installed is past both fixes, so nothing would be reported at all.
-- go.mod --
module example.com/foo

go 1.21.0
-- main.go --
package main

import "net/http"

func main() { http.Get("http://example.com/") }
-- sub/go.mod --
module example.com/bar

go 1.22.0
-- sub/sub.go --
package sub

import "net/http"

// Get is the call GO-TEST-9002 is against.
func Get() { http.Get("http://example.com/") }
-- want_diff.txt --
diff --git a/go.mod b/go.mod
index 9b981c3..d7e9510 100644
--- a/go.mod
+++ b/go.mod
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 module example.com/foo
 
-go 1.21.0
+go 1.21.9
diff --git a/sub/go.mod b/sub/go.mod
index 1de0b11..50e9640 100644
--- a/sub/go.mod
+++ b/sub/go.mod
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 module example.com/bar
 
-go 1.22.0
+go 1.22.5
-- want_report.md --
govulncheck found 2 vulnerabilities; this PR fixes 2:

**[GO-TEST-9001](https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-TEST-9001)**: Flood of requests in net/http

    net/http go1.21.0 -> go1.21.9

**[GO-TEST-9002](https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-TEST-9002)**: Mishandled redirect in net/http

    net/http go1.22.0 -> go1.22.5
